From 5ec5e90237a6f364d60ad44d2f2d9c08597b8a8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Fink <chris@cxjxf.com>
Date: Mon, 07 Sep 2026 07:44:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: size the Windows exception stack reserve from the host

Windows delivers exceptions on the faulting goroutine's own stack. Before
any handler runs, the kernel pushes an EXCEPTION_RECORD and a CONTEXT
below the faulting SP, and ntdll's dispatcher and sigtramp then run on
that same stack until sigtrampgo switches to g0. stackSystem reserves
4096 bytes below every goroutine stack for all of that.

The CONTEXT is not a fixed size. It carries the extended register state
(XSTATE) of every feature the OS enabled at boot, laid out even for
threads that never use it: about 1.7 KiB on a typical CPU, 3.4 KiB with
AVX-512, and 11.7 KiB on Intel Xeons with AMX, whose tile state alone is
8 KiB. On those hosts the frame overruns the bottom of the goroutine
stack by several KiB and corrupts whatever the heap put there; CL 824724
turns that into a throw, but the reserve is still too small.

Measure the reserve at startup instead of fixing it. InitializeContext
reports the size the kernel will push for this host, so osinit reserves
that plus 2048 bytes for the exception record, the kernel's alignment,
and the dispatcher frames below the CONTEXT (up to 1087 bytes measured,
on arm64). The minimum stack allocation and a Go-computed guard distance
move with the reserve. The compile-time stackGuard still applies where
the assembly bootstraps g0 before mstart0 and minit replace its guard,
and in the linker's nosplit check, which assumes only that floor. Hosts
whose need is below the floor are unchanged; AVX-512 hosts keep 8 KiB
minimum stacks with a larger guard; AMX hosts get 16 KiB minimum stacks.

The frame size is a property of the boot-time XSTATE mask: measured on
the affected hosts, a hardware fault's frame already includes the AMX
area before EnableProcessOptionalXStateFeatures and is unchanged after
it, so a startup measurement holds for the life of the process.

shrinkstack also needs the reserve: its quarter-full rule decides from
the live frames and the nosplit margin alone, and stackMinAlloc only
guarantees room for the reserve on a nearly empty stack. A goroutine
with more live frames than that could be shrunk to a stack with less
headroom below SP than the reserve promises, and resume there into an
exception before its next stack check. shrinkstack now also refuses a
shrink that would leave less than the reserve plus the nosplit margin
below the live frames.

Two runtime tests exercise this end to end, each re-running the test
binary in a subprocess since a too-small reserve crashes the process:
one recovers a real hardware fault after a goroutine's stack grows and
the GC shrinks it, on whatever reserve the host actually has; the other
forces a reserve and checks shrinkstack never leaves less than it below
SP, so that coverage does not depend on the builder's CPU having AMX.
Both fail if no shrink happened, rather than passing vacuously.

Fixes #81238

Change-Id: I46051a7d94205f85193615c2e37a29d5c025f67a
